WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.790
The investigation letters name units and features, not their age order.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:09.110
Granite G lies below Cambrian unit C, whose basal clasts came

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.470
from the granite. Ordovician B overlies C, and Permian A overlies

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:18.230
B across a disconformity. Jurassic S and Cretaceous K extend the

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.570
younger sedimentary record. Use fossils and contacts together.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.550
Fault F cuts the main sedimentary sequence in the field notes.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.190
Dike D cuts and bakes older units;

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.990
its relationship with the fault constrains their order in the supplied

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.310
terrain. Old river gravels R occupy channels that interrupt the dike.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:40.870
Cite the actual contact for each step instead of inventing a

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.130
universal event sequence.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.210
For granite G, the table gives one hundred twenty-five parents and

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:52.450
eight hundred seventy-five daughters. Under the exercise assumptions, the parent fraction

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.110
is one eighth of the original total.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:58.310
That means three half-lives. At five hundred million years per half-life,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.950
the calculated granite age is one point five billion years.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.790
For dike D, five hundred parents and five hundred daughters give

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:08.510
a parent fraction of one half.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:14.310
One half-life has elapsed. With the supplied forty-million-year half-life, the calculated

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.310
dike age is forty million years.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.070
This uses the table’s ideal dating assumptions;

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.730
the calculation does not make every nearby rock the same age.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:27.510
The narrow canyon cuts the older landscape and truncates an old

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.350
river channel. Lava L from volcano V then enters the already

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.870
existing canyon, constraining its relative order.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.850
The young volcanic surface has little soil, unlike the old gravel.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.590
Finish with a history supported step by step, retaining the original

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.830
diagram and the limits of each observation.
